      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "AWS IAM",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "TlzAoiJX8if5VNJ35KjZR",
      "originalText": "AWS IAM",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "BKqBaemM3dJlAVJuzwqOV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1954.9428483576967,
      "y": 10855.81246659308,
      "width": 1221,
      "height": 600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bHv",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1421475767,
      "version": 1769,
      "versionNonce": 1475695315,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785422289944,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Lets say we(as a company) have an AWS account, we have developers, we have devops team, we dont want\nto give credentials to everybody, because that can cause security issue, somebody can tamper with a\nlot of data, somebody can turn down all the services that we have, lot of things can happen.\nTo resove these kind of issues so that we can actually use services of AWS very easily in big organization,\nthere is a service provided by AWS called as IAM(Identity and access management).\nUsing this IAM service we can actually share diff permissions with users in our organization. There are\ngroup of people, to which we want to give them access to create an EC2 instance and manage everything\naround it but nothing else.\n\nThere is a group of people who will only manage Database instance nothing else, thats what IAM as a service\ndo.\nIAM manage users in your org by providing them releavant permission.\nIAM allows us to manage users and their level of access to the AWS console.\nWe can set users permissions and roles as well.\n\nLet say we have 7 users, with AWS IAM we dont need to give every user a set of permission, we can \ngroup people together for the same permissions, one user can belong to more that 1 group. and assign permission\nto those groups.\nDevelopers\nOperation people\nDatabase admins\n\nThese permissions are allocated using JSON documents called as policies. In this policies all the permissions\nmentioned in the JSON formate. ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Lets say we(as a company) have an AWS account, we have developers, we have devops team, we dont want\nto give credentials to everybody, because that can cause security issue, somebody can tamper with a\nlot of data, somebody can turn down all the services that we have, lot of things can happen.\nTo resove these kind of issues so that we can actually use services of AWS very easily in big organization,\nthere is a service provided by AWS called as IAM(Identity and access management).\nUsing this IAM service we can actually share diff permissions with users in our organization. There are\ngroup of people, to which we want to give them access to create an EC2 instance and manage everything\naround it but nothing else.\n\nThere is a group of people who will only manage Database instance nothing else, thats what IAM as a service\ndo.\nIAM manage users in your org by providing them releavant permission.\nIAM allows us to manage users and their level of access to the AWS console.\nWe can set users permissions and roles as well.\n\nLet say we have 7 users, with AWS IAM we dont need to give every user a set of permission, we can \ngroup people together for the same permissions, one user can belong to more that 1 group. and assign permission\nto those groups.\nDevelopers\nOperation people\nDatabase admins\n\nThese permissions are allocated using JSON documents called as policies. In this policies all the permissions\nmentioned in the JSON formate. ",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
