      "text": "Now let's go deep.\nSuppose you type: python app.py\n\nLet's follow every step.\nStep 1\n\nKeyboard sends characters.\n    p y t h o n -> terminal\n\n\nStep 2\nTerminal sends the command to the shell.\n\nUsually:\n    bash\n    or\n    zsh\n\nThe shell receives: python app.py\n\nStep 3\n\nShell searches PATH.\nRemember Day 1? \necho $PATH\n\nMaybe PATH contains:\n\n/usr/bin\n/bin\n/usr/local/bin\n\nThe shell checks:\n/usr/bin/python -> Found\n\n\nStep 4\n\nThe shell asks the kernel to execute /usr/bin/python (using the execve() system call)\nThis is where the operating system becomes involved.\n\nThe kernel:\n\nAllocates memory.\nCreates process metadata.\nAssigns a Process ID (PID).\nSets up resources (like file descriptors).\n\n\nStep 5\n\nThe kernel loads the Python executable into memory.\n    program will load from SSD to RAM\n\nNothing executes directly from the SSD.\nPrograms must first be loaded into RAM.\n\nStep 6\n\nPython starts running.\nPython now opens:\napp.py\n\nIt reads your code.\nCompiles it to bytecode internally.\nStarts executing it.\n\nStep 7\n\nWhenever Python needs something from the operating system, such as opening a file or \ncreating a network socket, it makes a system call to the kernel.\n\n\nFull Flow\n    Keyboard -> Terminal -> Shell -> PATH Lookup ->  Create Process -> Assign PID -> Load Python into RAM\n    -> Python Reads app.py -> Execute Code -> Output on Screen",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Now let's go deep.\nSuppose you type: python app.py\n\nLet's follow every step.\nStep 1\n\nKeyboard sends characters.\n    p y t h o n -> terminal\n\n\nStep 2\nTerminal sends the command to the shell.\n\nUsually:\n    bash\n    or\n    zsh\n\nThe shell receives: python app.py\n\nStep 3\n\nShell searches PATH.\nRemember Day 1? \necho $PATH\n\nMaybe PATH contains:\n\n/usr/bin\n/bin\n/usr/local/bin\n\nThe shell checks:\n/usr/bin/python -> Found\n\n\nStep 4\n\nThe shell asks the kernel to execute /usr/bin/python (using the execve() system call)\nThis is where the operating system becomes involved.\n\nThe kernel:\n\nAllocates memory.\nCreates process metadata.\nAssigns a Process ID (PID).\nSets up resources (like file descriptors).\n\n\nStep 5\n\nThe kernel loads the Python executable into memory.\n    program will load from SSD to RAM\n\nNothing executes directly from the SSD.\nPrograms must first be loaded into RAM.\n\nStep 6\n\nPython starts running.\nPython now opens:\napp.py\n\nIt reads your code.\nCompiles it to bytecode internally.\nStarts executing it.\n\nStep 7\n\nWhenever Python needs something from the operating system, such as opening a file or \ncreating a network socket, it makes a system call to the kernel.\n\n\nFull Flow\n    Keyboard -> Terminal -> Shell -> PATH Lookup ->  Create Process -> Assign PID -> Load Python into RAM\n    -> Python Reads app.py -> Execute Code -> Output on Screen",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
