      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffc9c9",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bIm",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1692698333,
      "version": 349,
      "versionNonce": 291053309,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785424840806,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "This is one of the most frequently asked Linux interview questions.\nLet's build it step by step.\n\nWhen Linux Boots\nWhen you press the power button:\n\nPower On -> BIOS / UEFI -> Bootloader (GRUB) -> Linux Kernel\n\nNow here's the interesting part.\nThe kernel is running.\nBut there are no user applications yet.\n\nNo\n\nSSH\nDocker\nNginx\nPostgreSQL\n\nNothing.\nThe kernel now needs to start all these services.\nWho is responsible?\n    systemd.\n\nsystemd is the init system and service manager for most modern Linux distributions.\nIt is typically the first user-space process started by the kernel.\n\nIts PID is usually: PID 1\n\nWhy Does Linux Need It?\n\nSuppose Linux boots.\nIt needs to start:\n    Network -> SSH -> Docker -> Cron -> Nginx -> PostgreSQL -> Redis\nSomeone has to coordinate all of this.\nThat's systemd.\n\n\nVisual\n    Power On -> BIOS -> GRUB -> Kernel -> systemd (PID 1) ->    │\n                                                                ├── SSH\n                                                                ├── Docker\n                                                                ├── PostgreSQL\n                                                                ├── Nginx\n                                                                ├── Redis\n                                                                ├── Cron\n                                                                └── User Login\n\nEverything ultimately traces back to systemd.\n\nWhat Does systemd Do?\n    1. Starts Services\n        systemctl start nginx\n        systemd launches Nginx.\n\n    2. Stops Services\n        systemctl stop nginx\n\n    3. Restarts Services\n        systemctl restart nginx\n\n    4. Automatically Starts Services on Boot\n        For example:\n            Docker, Redis, PostgreSQL\n            can be configured to start automatically whenever the machine boots.\n\n    5. Monitors Services\n        If a service crashes:\n            PostgreSQL -> Crash -> systemd ->  Restart PostgreSQL\n        This improves reliability.\n\n    6. Maintains Logs\n            journalctl\n        you can inspect logs collected by systemd's journal.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "This is one of the most frequently asked Linux interview questions.\nLet's build it step by step.\n\nWhen Linux Boots\nWhen you press the power button:\n\nPower On -> BIOS / UEFI -> Bootloader (GRUB) -> Linux Kernel\n\nNow here's the interesting part.\nThe kernel is running.\nBut there are no user applications yet.\n\nNo\n\nSSH\nDocker\nNginx\nPostgreSQL\n\nNothing.\nThe kernel now needs to start all these services.\nWho is responsible?\n    systemd.\n\nsystemd is the init system and service manager for most modern Linux distributions.\nIt is typically the first user-space process started by the kernel.\n\nIts PID is usually: PID 1\n\nWhy Does Linux Need It?\n\nSuppose Linux boots.\nIt needs to start:\n    Network -> SSH -> Docker -> Cron -> Nginx -> PostgreSQL -> Redis\nSomeone has to coordinate all of this.\nThat's systemd.\n\n\nVisual\n    Power On -> BIOS -> GRUB -> Kernel -> systemd (PID 1) ->    │\n                                                                ├── SSH\n                                                                ├── Docker\n                                                                ├── PostgreSQL\n                                                                ├── Nginx\n                                                                ├── Redis\n                                                                ├── Cron\n                                                                └── User Login\n\nEverything ultimately traces back to systemd.\n\nWhat Does systemd Do?\n    1. Starts Services\n        systemctl start nginx\n        systemd launches Nginx.\n\n    2. Stops Services\n        systemctl stop nginx\n\n    3. Restarts Services\n        systemctl restart nginx\n\n    4. Automatically Starts Services on Boot\n        For example:\n            Docker, Redis, PostgreSQL\n            can be configured to start automatically whenever the machine boots.\n\n    5. Monitors Services\n        If a service crashes:\n            PostgreSQL -> Crash -> systemd ->  Restart PostgreSQL\n        This improves reliability.\n\n    6. Maintains Logs\n            journalctl\n        you can inspect logs collected by systemd's journal.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "5nO32ancN-Pw2vwq-gb0r",
      "type": "text",
